# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Local development environment for the console (the BFF).
#
# `make env-scaffold` copies this to `web/.env.local`, which Next loads
# automatically and git ignores. The Rust server reads `.env` at the repo
# root; this file is the console's half and the two must agree on the ports.
#
# A NEXT_PUBLIC_ PREFIX SHIPS THE VALUE TO THE BROWSER. Next inlines every one
# of them into the client bundle, so the prefix is a publication decision, not
# a naming convention. Never give the server's URL one: the browser talks to
# the console and to nothing else.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Session sealing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The iron-session password for the sealed HttpOnly cookies: the session
# itself, the PKCE verifier, and the in-flight connector handshake. Sealed
# rather than signed, because the session holds the identity provider's tokens
# and nothing that gets the cookie should be able to read them.
#
# At least 32 bytes, enforced at boot. Changing it invalidates every live
# session at once, which is a blunt but real revocation lever.
AUTH_SECRET=dev-auth-secret-change-me-needs-32-bytes

# The console's own origin. Every redirect the console builds is absolute
# against this: the sign-in return, the connector callback landing, the invite
# link. It must match APP_URL (and OIDC_REDIRECT_URI's origin) in the root
# `.env`, or sign-in redirects the browser somewhere this process is not
# listening.
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Who runs this deployment ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# All optional, and the console names no operator without them: the company
# on the copyright line (the product's name otherwise), the address the
# footer offers, and the base under which YOUR privacy policy and terms are
# published (`{LEGAL_URL}/privacy-policy`, `{LEGAL_URL}/terms-of-service`).
# `/legal/*` on this console redirects there; unset, it answers 404. The
# auth service reads SUPPORT_EMAIL too, for the address its mail names.
COMPANY_NAME=
SUPPORT_EMAIL=
LEGAL_URL=

# The identity provider's origins, space separated, for the Content Security
# Policy's `form-action`: signing out from the account menu is a form post
# that ends in a redirect to the provider, which the browser holds to that
# directive, so a provider missing here is a sign-out that dies at the first
# hop (sign-in is a link, which the directive does not govern). Nothing else
# about the provider lives
# in the console; auth holds its credentials and mints every URL. Unset,
# only this site is allowed, which is all the accounts auth holds itself
# need: their sign-in pages are the console's own.
AUTH_PROVIDER_ORIGINS=

# ── Where the server is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one Rust process: auth and notifications as modules of a single binary
# on a single port, an internal-only workload the console alone can reach.
# Every call carries SERVICE_SECRET and the session's bearer token; nothing
# the browser runs may hold either.
#
# A HARD dependency — with it unreachable the console serves nothing, because
# `/me` is the one door a session cannot start without. The notification
# surfaces still degrade on their own: their fetchers return null on a failed
# call and the page renders its empty state.
SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8082

# ── Service trust ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must be byte-identical to SERVICE_SECRET in the root `.env`. It proves a hop
# began inside the platform and says nothing about who is asking: that is the
# session's own bearer, relayed as `Authorization` on every person call and
# resolved by auth, which minted it.
SERVICE_SECRET=

# ── Redis ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The console's store, and nobody else's: the Rust server never touches it. Four
# namespaces, every one expendable — sliding-window rate limits, the session
# blacklist, the server-sent-events pub/sub, and the announcement banner.
# Nothing here is a source of truth; every key is derived from Postgres or is a
# hint, which is what lets the whole store fail open.
#
# Unreachable, and: rate limits degrade to per-replica in memory, session
# revocation stops propagating between replicas, and live updates stop. The
# next navigation is still correct.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# REDIS_CA_CERT — optional, and unset locally. Memorystore signs with a
# per-instance CA no system trust store holds, so a managed deployment needs it
# or the rediss:// handshake fails verification and the limiter falls back to
# in-memory SILENTLY. Docker's Redis needs no TLS, so leave it out here.

# ── Proxies in front ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How many entries the proxies in front of the console append to
# X-Forwarded-For, counted from the end: that entry is the one client address
# no caller wrote, and the per-address rate limits (sign-in, sign-up, the
# reset, /v1, /cli) key on it. Google's load balancer appends the client's
# address and then its own, so the chart sets 2; one reverse proxy is 1;
# nothing in front is 0, which leaves those limits to the caller's word and
# is said at boot. Unset is 2, which `make up` — nothing in front, one entry
# — reads correctly too.
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=

# ── Testing behind a tunnel (Slack) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Slack insists on HTTPS for its redirect and its events URL, so testing it
# locally means the console is reached at a public hostname — a named
# Cloudflare tunnel, https://local.example.com → localhost:3000. Everything
# that names the origin then moves together: AUTH_URL above becomes
# https://local.example.com; APP_URL and OIDC_REDIRECT_URI in
# the root .env do too; the origin is registered at the identity provider as
# a redirect URI and a post-logout redirect URI, and at Slack; and this
# names the bare host so `next dev` serves its own assets to a page loaded from
# it (Next refuses cross-origin dev requests otherwise — the page renders and
# every script 403s). Unset on a deployment, where there is no dev server for
# it to mean anything to.
DEV_TUNNEL_HOST=

# ── Logging ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pino's level. The console emits the same JSON shape the Rust server does, with
# the keys Cloud Logging reserves, so one query reads both sides of a request.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── Optional, and unset on purpose ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Documented rather than declared, because a blank value here is indistinguishable
# from an absent one and none of these has a useful local default.
#
#   ANALYTICS_INGEST_URL / ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY
#       The third-party analytics sink. Both set, the privacy page offers the
#       consent switch; unset, it offers none, since nothing would be sent.
#       `track()` writes its log line either way and reaches the provider ONLY
#       when a call site passes consent explicitly, on top of the account's own
#       opt-in, which starts false. The default is false so a call site that
#       forgets fails closed.
#
#   TELMONI_ANNOUNCEMENT / NEXT_PUBLIC_TELMONI_ANNOUNCEMENT
#       The console-wide banner, as a fallback when Redis holds no
#       `telmoni:announcement` key. Operator-set; the Redis key wins.
#
#   ALLOW_TEST_SESSION
#       Opens `/api/test/session`, which signs a person in without the
#       sign-in page — the e2e suite's door. Gated on the literal string
#       "true" and it must stay unset anywhere real, since it is an
#       authentication bypass by design. It asks the server's own door,
#       which opens only when the server runs with the same variable, or it
#       answers 502; `ALLOW_TEST_SESSION=true make up` opens both.
